> Can you explain where L is coming from?

`L` comes from the Leaflet script you load in `static/index.html`:

```html
<script src="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.js"></script>
```

That file creates a global variable named `L` on the browser’s `window` object. So after Leaflet loads, these are basically the same:

```js
L.map("guessMap")
```

and:

```js
window.L.map("guessMap")
```

Leaflet uses `L` as its short namespace. All the Leaflet functions live under it:

```js
L.map(...)
L.tileLayer(...)
L.marker(...)
L.latLng(...)
```

One important detail: your own script must load **after** Leaflet:

```html
<script src="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.js"></script>
<script src="js/guess-map.js"></script>
```

If `guess-map.js` runs before Leaflet loads, then `L` will be undefined.

> What is the difference between L.map and L.tileLayer?

`L.map(...)` creates the **interactive map object**.

```js
const guessMap = L.map("guessMap").setView([20, 0], 2);
```

That means: “Turn the HTML element with `id="guessMap"` into a Leaflet map.”
This gives you the thing users can pan, zoom, and click.

`L.tileLayer(...)` creates the **map imagery/background layer**.

```js
L.tileLayer("https://{s}.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png", {
  attribution: "&copy; OpenStreetMap contributors",
}).addTo(guessMap);
```

That means: “Use OpenStreetMap image tiles as the visible map.”

So the relationship is:

```js
const guessMap = L.map("guessMap");      // the interactive canvas
const tiles = L.tileLayer("...");        // the visible map images

tiles.addTo(guessMap);                   // put the images onto the map
```

Without `L.map`, there is no Leaflet map.

Without `L.tileLayer`, the map still exists, but it would look blank because it has no background imagery.

