> How would I keep the guess submit button disabled until a guess is made?

Yep, exactly. Use the button’s built-in `disabled` property.

At the top, after selecting the button:

```js
const submitGuessButton = document.querySelector("#submitGuessBtn");
submitGuessButton.disabled = true;
```

Then, when the user clicks the map and you save `currentGuess`, enable it:

```js
function handleGuessClick(event) {
  const lat = event.latlng.lat;
  const lng = event.latlng.lng;

  currentGuess = { lat, lng };

  submitGuessButton.disabled = false;

  // marker logic here
}
```

Your submit handler can still keep the safety check:

```js
submitGuessButton.addEventListener("click", () => {
  if (!currentGuess) {
    console.log("Please guess on the map before submitting");
    return;
  }

  console.log("Guess submitted:", currentGuess);
});
```

So the flow is:

```js
let currentGuess = null;

const submitGuessButton = document.querySelector("#submitGuessBtn");
submitGuessButton.disabled = true;

// later, inside map click handler:
currentGuess = { lat, lng };
submitGuessButton.disabled = false;
```

One small CSS improvement: you can style disabled buttons so it’s visually obvious:

```css
.btn:disabled {
  opacity: 0.5;
  cursor: not-allowed;
}
```

> how would i raise an error to throw that the user has not guessed something yet?

You *can* throw an error with:

```js
throw new Error("Please guess on the map before submitting.");
```

In your handler:

```js
submitGuessButton.addEventListener("click", () => {
  if (!currentGuess) {
    throw new Error("Please guess on the map before submitting.");
  }

  console.log("Guess submitted:", currentGuess);
});
```

But for this specific case, I would **not** use `throw`.

A missing guess is not really a programmer error. It is an expected user action/state. Better options are:

```js
if (!currentGuess) {
  console.log("Please guess on the map before submitting.");
  return;
}
```

or later:

```js
if (!currentGuess) {
  showMessage("Please click the map before submitting.");
  return;
}
```

Use `throw new Error(...)` when something has gone wrong in your code and execution should stop loudly. For example:

```js
const submitGuessButton = document.querySelector("#submitGuessBtn");

if (!submitGuessButton) {
  throw new Error("Submit guess button was not found.");
}
```

That one *is* a real error, because your HTML is missing something your JavaScript depends on.

So the rule of thumb:

```js
// Expected user behavior
return;

// Broken program assumption
throw new Error(...);
```
